            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>2. Factors to Consider When Choosing Cabinet Styles</h2>
              <p>While the look of the cabinets is certainly important in 2026, it's also vital to consider several functional factors when selecting cabinet styles. These will ensure that your cabinetry not only looks great but serves its purpose for years to come.</p>

              <h3>Space and Layout</h3>
              <p>The layout of your kitchen or bathroom in 2026 should influence your cabinet style. For smaller spaces, flat-panel or Shaker cabinets with light finishes can help create the illusion of more space. Conversely, if you have a large, open-plan area, you may have more freedom to explore raised panel or inset cabinets for a bold statement.</p>

              <h3>Budget</h3>
              <p>Cabinet styles vary widely in terms of cost. While Shaker and flat-panel cabinets tend to be more affordable due to their simple construction, inset and raised panel cabinets are more labor-intensive to make and typically come with a higher price tag. See <a href="#cost-by-style">Section 4: Cabinet Style &amp; Cost</a> below for a detailed breakdown of how each style affects your Delaware County project budget. Budget considerations should always factor into your decision, especially if you're remodeling and need to balance style with affordability in 2026.</p>

              <h3>Material and Finish</h3>
              <p>The materials used for your cabinets will also impact the style you choose. Solid wood, such as maple, oak, or cherry, works well for traditional or rustic cabinet styles like raised panel or distressed cabinets. For a modern look, engineered wood or MDF (medium-density fiberboard) with a sleek lacquer finish can be a great match for flat-panel cabinets.</p>

              <h3>Storage Needs</h3>
              <p>Functionality is at the heart of cabinetry in 2026, so think about how the cabinets will be used. If you're looking to maximize storage in a small kitchen, Shaker or flat-panel cabinets might be ideal, as they can be configured with pull-out shelves, deep drawers, and built-in organizers. For larger kitchens, you might opt for more decorative raised panel cabinets, which can also include built-in storage solutions such as pull-out spice racks or lazy Susans.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- NEW SECTION 3: Delaware County Homeowners -->
            <div class="post-body__section post-body__section--new reveal" id="delaware-county-choices">
              <h2>3. What Delaware County Homeowners Are Actually Choosing &mdash; and Why</h2>
              <p>Generic cabinet guides tell you what Shaker is. They don't tell you what families in Media, Swarthmore, and Haverford are actually putting in their kitchens &mdash; or why. After completing kitchen and custom cabinetry projects across Delaware County and the Main Line, we've seen clear patterns emerge in what homeowners choose and the reasoning behind it.</p>

              <h3>Shaker Remains the Dominant Choice</h3>
              <p>In our Delaware County kitchen projects, Shaker-style cabinets come up most consistently &mdash; and this tracks with what we see market-wide: Shaker remains the top choice across Delaware County and Chester County in 2026 (per our <a href="/blog/kitchen-cabinets-cost/">local cost guide</a>). The reason has less to do with trend-chasing and more to do with architecture. Delaware County is home to a high concentration of colonial-era and mid-century homes. Shaker's recessed-panel geometry bridges that colonial DNA with modern stainless appliances and open-concept layouts without looking out of place in either direction.</p>

              <div class="post-case-study">
                <p class="post-case-study__label">ProBrothers Project &mdash; Kitchen</p>
                <h4 class="post-case-study__title">Two-Tone Kitchen Restoration</h4>
                <p>A client came to us wanting to transform an aging kitchen that needed a full gut-out &mdash; old cabinets, flooring, and appliances all out. The homeowner had seen two-tone kitchens online and wanted to know if the look was achievable at a realistic budget. The answer was yes: we built out a two-tone finish with a gray-and-white palette. The result was a cohesive upgrade that felt contemporary without losing warmth. The finish option &mdash; client-driven from the start &mdash; is something we see more often in Delaware County kitchens than in any other region we work in.</p>
              </div>

              <h3>Cabinet Style in Open-Concept Renovations</h3>
              <p>When Delaware County homeowners open up their floor plans &mdash; removing walls to connect a kitchen to a living or dining area &mdash; the cabinet style choice becomes more visible. With higher ceilings and continuous sightlines to adjacent rooms, some homeowners opt for flat-panel (slab) cabinetry for its clean, uninterrupted look; others keep a Shaker profile but simplify hardware and finish to read lighter at scale. The key is that the style has to work at room-wide distance, not just close up at the cabinet doors.</p>

              <div class="post-case-study">
                <p class="post-case-study__label">ProBrothers Project &mdash; Full Home Remodel, Media, PA</p>
                <h4 class="post-case-study__title">BlackHorse House &mdash; Media, PA</h4>
                <p>During a full home remodel in Media, the kitchen footprint doubled in size as walls came down to connect it directly to the living room. The expanded kitchen became the central design statement of the home &mdash; exactly what you'd expect when a kitchen goes from a closed-off prep area to the heart of the house. When a kitchen renovation involves this scale of spatial change, the cabinetry choice matters at the room level, not just the cabinet level.</p>
              </div>

              <h3>Custom Color as a Differentiator</h3>
              <p>One trend we see specifically in Delaware County's higher-end renovations is the move away from white-only kitchens toward deliberate color choices &mdash; deep blues, warm grays, sage greens. This isn't a paint-on-stock-cabinets decision; it requires a cabinet box and door construction that accepts premium paint well (MDF or furniture-grade plywood, factory primer, topcoat). When a homeowner wants a specific color that coordinates with custom metalwork or countertop material, the door style typically simplifies toward Shaker or flat-panel &mdash; because the color is the statement, not the millwork detail.</p>
            </div>

            <!-- NEW SECTION 4: Cost by Style -->
            <div class="post-body__section post-body__section--new reveal" id="cost-by-style">
              <h2>4. Cabinet Style &amp; Cost: Delaware County Breakdown</h2>
              <p>The style you choose affects both material and labor costs. Below is a practical breakdown of how each style maps to the cost tiers ProBrothers works with in the Delaware County and Main Line PA market. Full per-project pricing is covered in our <a href="/blog/kitchen-cabinets-cost/">Kitchen Cabinet Costs in Media, PA (2026 Guide)</a>.</p>

