                      <td>Popular in open-concept renovations. Wide range: affordable for budget projects, high-end for lacquer/glass-front designs.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td><strong>Raised Panel</strong></td>
                      <td>Semi-Custom to Custom<br /><small>$100&ndash;$1,200+/lin.&nbsp;ft installed</small></td>
                      <td>Multiple router passes per door; more finishing steps. Premium wood species (cherry, alder) add material cost. Not typically available in stock tier.</td>
                      <td>Suited to formal traditional Delaware County homes (Victorian, Colonial-era). Typically mid-to-upper budget.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td><strong>Inset</strong> <span class="post-cost-table__tag post-cost-table__tag--prem">Premium</span></td>
                      <td>Semi-Custom to Custom<br /><small>Upper semi-custom to $1,200+/lin.&nbsp;ft installed</small></td>
                      <td>Precision fitting: doors must seat flush inside the face frame with tight, even gaps. Labor-intensive; adds approximately 10&ndash;15% to a comparable overlay build.</td>
                      <td>Sought after in historic home restorations throughout Delaware County and West Chester. Furniture-grade result; long lead times.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                      <td><strong>Distressed</strong></td>
                      <td>Semi-Custom<br /><small>$100&ndash;$650/lin.&nbsp;ft installed</small></td>
                      <td>Base door (Shaker or raised panel) plus a hand-applied finishing premium (glazing, distress marks, crackle). The door itself isn't complex; the finish is the cost.</td>
                      <td>Used in farmhouse, rustic, or cottage-style renovations in the region. Not a dominant choice in Delaware County's traditional or contemporary markets.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                  <tfoot>
                    <tr>
                      <td colspan="4">Cost ranges per linear foot are installed rates (supply + labor) for the Delaware County / Main Line PA market. Full project cost depends on kitchen size, layout complexity, and hidden costs (demo, electrical, plumbing). See <a href="/blog/kitchen-cabinets-cost/">Kitchen Cabinet Costs in Media, PA</a> for full breakdowns.</td>
                    </tr>
                  </tfoot>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>

            <!-- NEW SECTION 5: From the Workshop -->
            <div class="post-body__section post-body__section--new reveal" id="from-workshop">
              <h2>5. From the Workshop: What Daniil Notices After 15+ Years of Installations</h2>
              <p>Cabinet guides cover what each style looks like. What they rarely address is what you discover once you're actually in a Delaware County home with a tape measure, a level, and 15 years of installation experience behind you. Here's what I've learned on the job.</p>

              <div class="post-workshop">
                <div class="post-workshop__header">
                  <div class="post-workshop__avatar" aria-hidden="true">DA</div>
                  <div>
                    <p class="post-workshop__name">Daniil Akulov</p>
                    <p class="post-workshop__byline">Founder &amp; Master Carpenter &mdash; ProBrothers Construction &mdash; Delaware County &amp; Main Line PA</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="post-workshop__body">
                  <p>The first thing I check when I walk into a Delaware County kitchen isn't the layout or the lighting &mdash; it's the walls. Older homes in Media, Swarthmore, and Haverford were built with plaster, not drywall. Plaster is harder, denser, and significantly less forgiving of imprecise anchoring. When you're hanging a row of upper cabinets on a plaster wall, you cannot afford to be off on your stud locations or your shimming. The cabinets look fine from 10 feet away if you're a fraction of an inch out; they look wrong from 3 feet away, and the homeowner will notice it every time they open the doors.</p>

                  <blockquote class="post-workshop__insight">"Shaker is the style I recommend most often &mdash; not because it's trendy, but because it's genuinely versatile. I've put Shaker cabinets in homes built in 1890 and homes built in 2008 and they've worked in both. No other style has that range."</blockquote>

                  <p><strong>On why Shaker dominates Delaware County kitchens:</strong> The colonial architecture of this region gives most homes a certain character &mdash; crown moldings, wainscoting, symmetrical windows. Shaker's recessed panel sits comfortably next to that tradition without looking like a museum piece. At the same time, pair it with modern hardware and a contemporary countertop and it doesn't fight the update. That's a rare combination. Flat-panel is cleaner, but it can look clinical in a home with a lot of traditional trim detail. Raised panel can look heavy in a kitchen that's received modern appliances and light-colored counters.</p>

                  <blockquote class="post-workshop__insight">"The Mid-Atlantic climate is genuinely hard on cabinetry. Humid summers, dry winters &mdash; wood moves. If you're doing solid-wood doors in a face-frame build, you need floating panel construction or you'll get cracking at the glue joints. I've seen beautiful raised-panel doors with cracks running down the panel within two winters because someone glued them up solid."</blockquote>

                  <p><strong>On inset cabinets:</strong> Inset is the most demanding install I do. The gap between the door and the face frame has to be even &mdash; top, bottom, both sides. I'm talking 1/16&quot; variation at most. On a new house with drywall and fresh framing, that's achievable. On a 1940s colonial in Swarthmore where the floor has settled over 80 years, you're shimming, adjusting, and re-checking constantly. Inset cabinetry in an older home costs more because it takes longer &mdash; not because the doors are more expensive, but because the installation is much more precise. Clients who want that furniture-grade look get it, but they need to go in with realistic expectations about timing and budget.</p>

                  <p><strong>On two-tone finishes:</strong> Two-tone kitchens have become genuinely popular in Delaware County over the past two years. The key thing homeowners need to understand is that a two-tone kitchen works best when the two colors are coordinated at the design stage, not chosen separately on site. I've seen projects where the upper cabinet color was picked first and the lower selected as an afterthought &mdash; and the result looked like a mistake. When it's planned from the start with the countertop and hardware in mind, it's one of the strongest visual outcomes I've seen in a residential kitchen.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>6. How Cabinet Style Complements Your Home's Overall Design</h2>
              <p>Cabinet style should harmonize with the other design elements in the room. For example, in a modern home with clean lines and minimalist decor, flat-panel cabinets with sleek hardware are a perfect fit. For more traditional homes, Shaker or raised panel cabinets can complement other classic features, such as crown molding, hardwood floors, and wainscoting, an aesthetic that remains timeless into 2026.</p>
              <p>It's also important to think about the color and finish of your cabinets in relation to the rest of the space. White or light-colored cabinets can open up a room, while dark cabinets add drama and richness. If you're working with a specific color scheme or design theme, choose a cabinet style that either contrasts or complements those tones.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>4. Tips for Finding the Best Cabinet Styles for Your Space</h2>
              <ul class="post-list">
                <li><strong>Match the Style to Your Home's Architecture:</strong> Choose a cabinet style that complements the architectural features of your home. For example, raised panel cabinets may be better suited for a colonial-style home, while flat-panel cabinets are perfect for a contemporary urban apartment.</li>
                <li><strong>Consider the Room's Purpose:</strong> A kitchen needs cabinets that are durable, easy to clean, and functional, whereas bathroom cabinets might prioritize compactness and storage. Be sure the style fits your space's specific requirements in 2026.</li>
                <li><strong>Think About Long-Term Appeal:</strong> While it's tempting to go for a trendy cabinet style, remember that cabinetry is a long-term investment. Choose a timeless style that will still look great in years to come.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>5. Conclusion: Finding the Perfect Cabinet Styles</h2>
              <p>Choosing the right cabinet style can be an exciting, yet daunting task, but by considering the architecture of your home, your space's needs, and your budget, you can make an informed decision. Whether you lean toward classic Shaker cabinets, the sleekness of flat-panel designs, or the elegance of raised panels, there's a perfect cabinet style out there for every home and every homeowner.</p>
              <p>Remember, cabinets aren't just storage solutions—they're an integral part of your home's overall design. So, choose wisely, and don't be afraid to ask for professional advice to ensure you get the best cabinetry for your project. Whether you're building new or remodeling, the right cabinet style will make your space functional, beautiful, and uniquely yours.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="post-body__section reveal">
              <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Custom Cabinetry and Kitchen Styles</h2>
              <div class="post-faq">
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>What is the most popular kitchen cabinet style for 2026?</h3>
                  <p><strong>Shaker-style cabinets</strong> remain the top choice for homeowners in 2026 due to their timeless, minimalist design. Their clean lines and recessed center panels make them versatile enough for both modern farmhouse and contemporary urban designs in the <strong>Greater Philadelphia area</strong>. They are also highly customizable with different hardware and paint finishes, ensuring long-term resale value.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Are inset cabinets worth the extra cost?</h3>
                  <p>Yes, <strong>inset cabinets</strong> are widely considered a premium investment because of their precise craftsmanship. Unlike overlay cabinets, the doors and drawers are fitted flush inside the cabinet frame. This creates a high-end, furniture-like appearance that is particularly sought after in historic home restorations throughout <strong>Delaware County and West Chester</strong>.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Which cabinet style is best for a small kitchen?</h3>
                  <p>For small kitchens, <strong>flat-panel (slab) or Shaker cabinets</strong> with a light-colored finish are best. Their lack of ornate detail prevents the space from looking cluttered, while light colors reflect natural light to make the room feel larger. In smaller homes across <strong>Media and Springfield, PA</strong>, we often recommend white or light gray cabinetry to maximize the sense of space.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>What is the difference between solid wood and MDF cabinet doors?</h3>
                  <p>Solid wood (like Maple or Oak) is prized for its durability and natural grain, making it ideal for stained finishes. However, <strong>MDF (Medium-Density Fiberboard)</strong> is often preferred for painted cabinets because it does not expand or contract with temperature changes, preventing the paint from cracking at the joints – a common issue in the humid Mid-Atlantic climate.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>How can I match my cabinets to my home's architecture?</h3>
                  <p>To ensure a cohesive look, match the cabinet detail to your home's era. For a <strong>Colonial or Victorian home in Pennsylvania</strong>, raised-panel or inset cabinets complement the architectural history. For a <strong>modern industrial loft in Philadelphia</strong>, flat-panel slab cabinets with minimalist hardware provide the sleek, streamlined aesthetic required for contemporary living.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="post-faq__item reveal">
                  <h3>Do custom cabinets increase home value more than stock cabinets?</h3>
                  <p>Absolutely. High-quality cabinetry is one of the first things buyers notice in a kitchen. Custom cabinets offer superior storage solutions, better materials, and a perfect fit for your specific layout. In competitive markets like <strong>Kennett Square and Malvern</strong>, custom cabinetry can significantly differentiate your home and provide a higher return on investment (ROI) during a renovation. Annual cost-return data from the <a href="https://www.remodeling.hw.net/cost-vs-value/2025/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Remodeling Magazine Cost vs. Value Report</a> consistently shows kitchen upgrades — including cabinetry — among the top ROI projects for homeowners in the Philadelphia metro market.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

